  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nThe central idea is to use a hierarchical optimization framework based on the fact that the optimal solution for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem exhibits multiple scales of structure. We will combine three techniques:\n1. Multi-resolution analysis - start from few points and iteratively increase resolution\n2. Bayesian optimization - adaptively select the most promising regions\n3. Response surface methodology - create surrogate models to guide searches\n\nThis approach is fundamentally different from traditional fixed-resolution optimization because:\na) It dynamically adjusts the problem size based on observed behavior\nb) It uses probabilistic modeling rather than pure gradient-based methods\nc) It leverages hierarchical structure to reduce the effective search space\n\nKey mathematical components:\na) Multi-scale representation: h(x) = \u03a3 a_i \u03c6_i(x) where {\u03c6_i} includes basis functions with varying smoothness\nb) Progressive hierarchy: optimization proceeds from low to high resolution\nc) Learned complexity: model complexity increases with the number of points\n\nBottlenecks addressed:\n1. Local optima - tackled by hierarchical jumps and Bayesian optimization\n2. Convergence speed - addressed by surrogate modeling\n3. Search space memory - handled by multi-resolution information transfer\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\na) Representation:\n- n-point samples over [0,2]\n- h(x): [0,1] for all x\n- \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n- C5 = max_k [\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx]\n\nb) Smoothed Objective Formulation:\nf_\u03b1(h) = (1/\u03b1) * log(\u2211_k exp(\u03b1*(c_k - m))) \nwhere c_k = \u2211_i h_i(1-h_{i+k])\nm = max_k c_k\n\nc) Evolutionary Hyperparameter Optimization:\n- Initial temperature \u03b8_init = 0.5\n- Cooling schedule: \u03b8 = \u03b8_min + (\u03b8_init - \u03b8_min) * (t/T_max)^\u03b2\n- \u03b2 \u2208 [1.5, 2.5] hyperparameter \u03b2 optimized online\n\nd) Dimensionality Adaptation:\n\u03c3_ij = (n + n_ref)/(n_effective) * \u03c3_0\nwhere \u03c3_0 is base standard deviation\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\na) Low-level population initialization:\n- Base resolution: n_base = 32 (to ensure feasibility)\n- Random uniform with clipping and sum adjustment\n- Repair mechanism: projected gradient descent\n\nb) Hierarchical Search:\nPhase1: Coarse resolution\n- n = n_base * 2^k increments\n- Bayesian optimization with EI acquisition func\n- Smoothed objective with \u03b1 = 5000\n- For each step: n_new = n_old * 2\n\nPhase2: Medium resolution\n- Active shifts selection: 0.01% wins\n- Response surface with Gaussian process surrogate\n- \u03b1 = 25000 for tighter approximation\n\nPhase3: Fine resolution\n- Bayesian adaptive control\n- Multi-fidelity evaluation\n- \u03b1 = 100000\n\nc) Evaluation Integration:\n- c5 = np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * (2.0/n)\n- Evolution: (-c5 executes\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\na) Weighted Backtracking:\n- Maintain set of top-k solutions from previous resolution \n- Use for initialization at next level\n\nb) Dynamic Smoothing:\n- \u03b1 adjustment based on curvature estimates\n\nc) Progressive Restart:\n- Restart using interpolated solutions for each doubling\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\na) Multi-resolution budget allocation:\n- Coarse: 25% budget\n- Medium: 50% budget\n- Finer: 25% budget\n\nb) Internal timing checkpoints:\n- At n=128: 400s\n- At n=256: 500s\n- At n=512: 900s\n- At n=1024: 1100s (stop)\n\nc) Fallback mechanisms:\n- Use n=512 as default if any level exceeds time\n- Repair using projected gradient\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\na) Post-optimization checks:\n- 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n- abs(sum(h) - n/2) < n/2 * 1e-5 * dx\n- Compute C5 using np.correlate\n\nb) Final validation:\n- Re-score proposed solution with original method\n\nc) Acceptance criterion:\n- 20% improvement over previous best or time limit\n\nThe multidimensional nature of the problem requires careful attention:\n- Actual impact: smoother found 0.38075\n- Theoretical improvement possible if implemented well\n\nThis plan integrates mathematical insight with probabilistic methods to escape local optima while leveraging the hierarchical structure of the problem. The discrete nature of the solution, together with multi-resolution optimization, shifts the approach away from pure calculus-based methods.\n\nNote: Implementation assumes scipy.optimize.minimize can handle 500-dimensional smooth optimization with the BFGS method. The lower-level code must ensure projected gradient repair and smooth objective calculation is properly implemented. Up to 58 degree-of-freedom might require density smoothing.",
